From: ghc-darwin8-ppc project
Subject: [PATCH] rts/Linker.c: match Mach-O underscore-prefixed ___dso_handle

ghc-darwin8-ppc: `rts/Linker.c::lookupDependentSymbol` has a special
case for `__dso_handle` (see `Note [Resolving __dso_handle]`, upstream
#20493) that hands the dependent object's image address back as a
synthetic handle.  The strcmp was written for ELF, where the symbol
arrives with two leading underscores.

Mach-O preserves the platform's leading-underscore symbol-name prefix.
Our PPC Mach-O loader populates `macho_symbols[i].name` directly from
the object's string table (`rts/linker/MachO.c:137-138`), so the
symbol arrives at `lookupDependentSymbol` as `"___dso_handle"` (three
underscores).  The strcmp misses, the special case never fires, and
resolution falls through to `dlsym(lbl + 1)`.  On Tiger this also
fails -- `___dso_handle` is provided at link time by `dylib1.o`/`crt1.o`
and is not exported into the runtime dyld namespace.  The .o load
therefore aborts with:

    lookupSymbol failed in resolveImports
    /tmp/.../T9878b.o: unknown symbol `___dso_handle'

The fix is to match both spellings.  Same semantics on ELF and Mach-O
(the loader hands the dependent object's image back either way); only
the spelling at lookup time differs.

Surfaced by session 60's extended ghci-tnum runner on T9878b
(`-fobject-code` + `:l T9878b.hs` with `import GHC.StaticPtr; static
True` -- SPT init emits a call to `__cxa_atexit(..., __dso_handle)`).
Released as v0.14.2.

diff --git a/rts/Linker.c b/rts/Linker.c
index 19545fd3..4c06a477 100644
--- a/rts/Linker.c
+++ b/rts/Linker.c
@@ -851,7 +851,8 @@ SymbolAddr* lookupDependentSymbol (SymbolName* lbl, ObjectCode *dependent)
     RtsSymbolInfo *pinfo;

     /* See Note [Resolving __dso_handle] */
-    if (strcmp(lbl, "__dso_handle") == 0) {
+    if (strcmp(lbl, "__dso_handle") == 0 ||
+        strcmp(lbl, "___dso_handle") == 0) { /* Mach-O underscore prefix */
         if (dependent) {
             return dependent->image;
         } else {
@@ -925,6 +926,11 @@ SymbolAddr* lookupDependentSymbol (SymbolName* lbl, ObjectCode *dependent)
  * relocations of bounded displacement and therefore __dso_handle must not be
  * too far from the loaded object's code (hence using its start address).
  *
+ * On Mach-O the symbol table preserves the leading underscore prefix
+ * convention, so the symbol arrives at lookupDependentSymbol() as
+ * "___dso_handle" (three underscores) rather than the ELF spelling
+ * "__dso_handle" (two).  We match both forms above.
+ *
  * See #20493.
  */
